Emulate glEdgeFlag for polygon-mode lines.

gl4es stubs glEdgeFlag to a no-op (src/gl/wrap/glstub.c), so under
glPolygonMode(..., GL_LINE) every edge of every polygon is drawn: the
boundary edges that edge flags exist to suppress all come back. That hits
gl-repl's wireframe, hidden-line, vertex-outline and polygon-highlight
views, and the GLU tessellator, which wires GLU_TESS_EDGE_FLAG straight to
glEdgeFlag so a tessellated concave polygon keeps a clean outline instead of
showing every interior diagonal. The failure is silent -
globals4es.silentstub defaults on, so nothing is printed.

glGetBooleanv(GL_EDGE_FLAG) was unhandled too: it fell through to GLES and
raised GL_INVALID_ENUM without writing the parameter.

The flag is consumed entirely on the CPU - it decides which polygon-mode
line edges exist - so it never becomes a vertex attribute and never reaches
the GPU.

Storage
-------

renderlist_t grows a GLubyte *edgeflag, one byte per vertex. NULL means
"every vertex is GL_TRUE", so geometry that never calls glEdgeFlag(GL_FALSE)
- which is nearly all of it - allocates nothing and costs one predictable
null check per vertex in rlVertexCommon(). It is deliberately kept out of the
interleaved merger_master layout, which is exactly full at 20 floats per
vertex and would otherwise grow for every vertex in the process.

Generation
----------

This cannot be done by filtering fill_lineIndices()'s output. That function
works from the already-triangulated index array, so by the time it runs a
quad has become two triangles: it emits the interior diagonal as a real edge,
and it emits the pairs unoriented - a GL_QUADS block comes out as
(0,1) (0,2) (1,2) (0,3) (2,3), where the boundary edge 3->0 appears as (0,3).
Neither "which vertex starts this edge" nor "is this edge a boundary at all"
survives that, so a filter over those pairs suppresses the wrong edges. (It
was written that way first; the coverage oracle caught it - clearing vertex
0's flag removed three edges instead of one, and clearing vertex 3's removed
none.)

The vertices themselves are still in specification order - triangulation
reorders the indices, not the array - so flagged geometry gets its edges
built by build_lineIndices_edgeflag() straight from the original primitive
topology. modeinit_t gains a vlen field recording each block's cumulative
vertex span, because ilen is in index space once a list carries indices and
no longer marks the primitive grouping.

GL_TRIANGLES, GL_QUADS, GL_QUAD_STRIP and GL_POLYGON are reconstructed - each
emits at most 2 indices per vertex, inside the caller's 4-per-vertex
allocation.  Triangle strips and fans return -1 and fall back to
fill_lineIndices() with the flags ignored, as before: their edge count is not
bounded by that allocation, and they are not where edge flags are used.

A side effect, called out because it is visible: flagged geometry also loses
the triangulation diagonal that fill_lineIndices() draws inside a quad or
polygon. That is what GL does - the diagonal is an internal edge, not a
boundary - so flagged geometry gets the more correct outline. Unflagged
geometry keeps gl4es's existing behavior; fixing that everywhere is a
separate change, and the oracle pins the current split so it cannot drift
unnoticed.

Odds and ends
-------------

glEdgeFlag needs no packed-call entry for display lists: a compiling list
picks the flag up through its own lastEdgeFlag, and a freshly extended one
through the copied lastEdgeFlag state. Executing or
appending a list propagates its tail lastEdgeFlag back into the current state,
so subsequent immediate vertices and glGetBooleanv(GL_EDGE_FLAG) see the same
value as native GL. glEdgeFlagv is promoted out of the stub table alongside
it. GL_CURRENT_BIT now saves and restores the flag.

The query side needs both halves.  GL_EDGE_FLAG joins gl4es_commonGet(),
which serves glGetIntegerv/glGetFloatv/glGetDoublev; glGetBooleanv had no
gl4es-side implementation at all, so it gets one (skip_glGetBooleanv) that
answers GL_EDGE_FLAG and passes every other pname straight to the driver,
exactly as before.  Answering the rest of gl4es's tracked state locally is a
worthwhile optimization but a separate decision - see
gl4es-getbooleanv-local-state.patch.

The client-array path (glEdgeFlagPointer) stays stubbed - gl-repl draws in
immediate mode, so renderlists cover it.

Verified with packaging/web/bench/gl4es_edge_flag.c (`make bench-web-gl4es`,
headless Chrome/SwiftShader): each of a quad's four and a triangle's three
suppressions lands within a pixel of the calibrated edge it should remove,
and gl4es-line-width reproduces its documented pre-patch numbers exactly.

